{"title":"1998 CEMC Gauss (Grade 8) Problems/Problem 20","text":"<div class=\"mw-content-ltr mw-parser-output\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Problem\">Problem</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1998_CEMC_Gauss_(Grade_8)_Problems/Problem_20&amp;action=edit&amp;section=1\" title=\"Edit section: Problem\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Three playing cards are placed in a row. The club is to the right of the heart and the diamond. The 5 is to the left of the heart. The 8 is to the right of the 4. From left to right, the cards are\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/9/0/d90ee8ae250f887d76de8bb30ae95662a6083807.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\text{(A)} \\text{4 of hearts, 5 of diamonds, 8 of clubs} \\quad\\text{(B)} \\text{5 of diamonds, 4 of hearts, 8 of clubs} \\quad \\text{(C)} \\text{8 of clubs, 4 of hearts, 5 of diamonds} \\quad \\text{(D)} \\text{4 of diamonds, 5 of clubs, 8 of hearts} \\quad \\text{(E)} \\text{5 of hearts, 4 of diamonds, 8 of clubs} \\quad$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"1556\" height=\"18\" />\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_1\">Solution 1</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1998_CEMC_Gauss_(Grade_8)_Problems/Problem_20&amp;action=edit&amp;section=2\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 1\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>If the club is to the right of the heart and the diamond, it must be all the way to the right because there are only <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/c/d/7cde695f2e4542fd01f860a89189f47a27143b66.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$3$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> cards. This means that the first two cards must be the heart and diamond as well:\n</p><p>__(unknown number) of Clubs\n</p><p>Since the 5 is to the left of the heart and there's only two spaces remaining, we can conclude that the 5 must be the diamond and that it must be the leftmost card. The middle card must also be the heart because it's the only remaining space for the hearts.\n</p><p>5 of diamond (unknown number of heart) (unknown number of clubs)\n</p><p>We can now use the fact that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/4/5/8455f3b5cb3b4880b8c9d782a5c1f0334db819eb.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$8$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> is to the right of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/7/c/c7cab1a05e1e0c1d51a6a219d96577a16b7abf9d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$4$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" />. There are only two spaces left, so we see that the <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/4/5/8455f3b5cb3b4880b8c9d782a5c1f0334db819eb.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$8$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> must be the clubs while <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/7/c/c7cab1a05e1e0c1d51a6a219d96577a16b7abf9d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$4$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> must be the heart. \n</p><p>Combining everything together, we see that the <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/b/d/0bdc3df06c9ec5d5b66164f3eac35fd85cd0039a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\boxed {\\textbf {(B) } \\text{5 of diamonds, 4 of hearts, 8 of clubs}}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -11px\" width=\"317\" height=\"30\" /> arrangement must be the correct answer.\n</p><p>~anabel.disher\n</p>\n<h2><span id=\"Solution_2_.28answer_choices.29\"></span><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_2_(answer_choices)\">Solution 2 (answer choices)</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1998_CEMC_Gauss_(Grade_8)_Problems/Problem_20&amp;action=edit&amp;section=3\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 2 (answer choices)\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>We can first reach the same conclusion about the club first, like in solution 1, and then the same conclusion about how <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/9/0/79069377f91364c2f87a64e5f9f562a091c8a6c1.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$5$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> of diamonds must be the leftmost card.\n</p><p>However, we can notice that the only answer choice where <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/9/0/79069377f91364c2f87a64e5f9f562a091c8a6c1.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$5$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> of diamonds is the leftmost card is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/b/d/0bdc3df06c9ec5d5b66164f3eac35fd85cd0039a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\boxed {\\textbf {(B) } \\text{5 of diamonds, 4 of hearts, 8 of clubs}}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -11px\" width=\"317\" height=\"30\" />\n</p><p>~anabel.disher\n</p>\n<table class=\"wikitable\" style=\"margin:0.5em auto; font-size:95%; border:1px solid black; width:80%;\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td style=\"background:#ccf;text-align:center;\" colspan=\"3\"><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1998_CEMC_Gauss_(Grade_8)\" title=\"1998 CEMC Gauss (Grade 8)\">1998 CEMC Gauss (Grade 8)</a></b> (<b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1998_CEMC_Gauss_(Grade_8)_Problems&amp;action=edit&amp;redlink=1\" class=\"new\" title=\"1998 CEMC Gauss (Grade 8) Problems (page does not exist)\">Problems</a></b> • <b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1998_CEMC_Gauss_(Grade_8)_Answer_Key&amp;action=edit&amp;redlink=1\" class=\"new\" title=\"1998 CEMC Gauss (Grade 8) Answer Key (page does not exist)\">Answer Key</a></b> • <a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external text\" href=\"https://cemc.math.uwaterloo.ca/contests/past_contests.html\">Resources</a>)\n</td></tr>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"50%\" align=\"center\" rowspan=\"1\">Preceded&#160;by<br /><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1998_CEMC_Gauss_(Grade_8)_